[2025-04-15 21:50:34,384][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-2 << "kKI74fYpjqQvP-wtpYwzgSNtYbriOj_Inx6oAxXN3exFZYy-PE_1fMb836kTNqlhFh5PGMQlU4PS3ofhgRSEELjVu9mR048wnKigUshpLTWGVL2fA/4fj/hW0Zlr8VTFSnksh5tATcKQ/h18/h001.j1epidDPqlkyfj4DvEAVSPVKUZvGZcS_sg-YByzk9wk&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Accidentally Expose All Your Stuff on S3 with a Bucket Policy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Another excellent Cloud Security Lab a Week post by &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.-QQnKmN9995ElcpR565Sh-SDvgyu6m58HhCZ9db8DGLaMpadp4zQzoKSqSCFZLnu6DRx1ZCeRHT_BZhm-ZvzkRGM3ljSAMqRV0lt1dKT69DXlM5QIReTr9tUymfuTfZVz53fpOQgzm05ouh6bbgI64l8gyCF6-hTMtOVOyS_rshMaC7qnng35q2h39qwy5Z58LOx4bLb6_6PMNQl8qIn_BPwkrTy3tUr6iDVLRWjmNxM-7qaNGUmpv-zMcKZmcHJhFlRJ_fHm2WrmvJXCYNC5b7EpsRIEE4pM2YN0w_Qdk95AVWgcXCGGgboiJWKVvI7jeP6ggv94TDRitviXXje-g/4fj/hW0Zlr8VTFSnksh5tATcKQ/h19/h001.Jq018SFA194-W2xbUqxKZR-wXdjsfUMSKJCvPNezAhg&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rich Mogull&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;, who explains how S3 bucket policies work. He discusses policy evaluation logic, highlighting that resource policies are evaluated before identity policies, and can allow access even without an explicit allow in identity policies. [0xe2][0x80][0x9c]The practical application is that if you give a role access to a bucket in the bucket policy, unless there[0xe2][0x80][0x99]s a deny statement, they get access [0xe2][0x80][0x94] even if that role doesn[0xe2][0x80][0x99]t have access to S3 in its IAM policy.[0xe2][0x80][0x9d]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The post shares an example bucket policy to show some of the nuances, and demonstrates two ways to make a bucket public: allowing access from any principal and allowing access from any IP address.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.-QQnKmN9995ElcpR565Shz-FkwPqTW6hJuIjzacjKzFf8zg8OtZ6rTlmKBR1YlSZ6WS8Y0IZ_pvr_IC2SodyKMKjWj6OIVGVpOSjocAElrPsedLDOTpOdN61zMrvI39PS4Vscqm_I-01jNl4SNgHoY3ov05waZ9HzXD_R9UU438sAxlxpo_duzCCug3OOGUmOgilHImpgI_ApvUBDYw_ddUPgk5838s218Kjm_T1Z0KGp33wnTd46Fg9e5qasVaQKRk-ze1gei6R4auqQnP19LVu6N8S-t7T2DHnVqE7kQLCmv5-TwsoTSMxZSa9A6tcmksRF0_t8K88Mh-npxWNsDG1nTr6LavmvYf3926fKgT-jFxisJFB88ahkdVDPCUZz9HWEVBXYcmXBDQasActFg/4fj/hW0Zlr8VTFSnksh5tATcKQ/h20/h001.wcJHKrYF8EaCCYxQ41S0tg8mUprdUF_XW-B0d-hOw80&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Invoking Misconfigured API Gateways from Any External AWS Accounts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;OffensAI[0xe2][0x80][0x99]s &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.-QQnKmN9995ElcpR565Sh-SDvgyu6m58HhCZ9db8DGIfJsTOGlv-OfESNH6tS1QQmfqJ1vtG6nuD-AoI7CUsU3Rsq0TTrTet6F21wSAg12mpKhnPVQboe4uv64kZi8CfqV2oOKZs0fbKRg8Gh36_oNK5WEIbWdTJ0NBNEnrtwf1FT0Twi7WS3ZDHbDgLkjaRpYnXXJOmAfcmPSgPWFbC378dKCSpKJHh22C3bwONfWrhF8E6mQkSRKyE9W_VsIS2zKK2oBm_TIEUoV6xobagZE1sjv9MWLc87alQHKzs5O8x6ut3EIkatMMYbPjd9DPF4opDHTYqWjN25dfld9c67A/4fj/hW0Zlr8VTFSnksh5tATcKQ/h21/h001.16z2NK30fQGsD5Ppo6PVQA6ZUPRWUIBtwy5GEZJH42U&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Eduard Agavriloae&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; explains how &amp;quot;private&amp;quot; API Gateways in AWS can be exploited if misconfigured, allowing access from any AWS account. The attack involves creating a VPC endpoint and EC2 instance in the same region as the target API, then sending HTTP requests through the VPC endpoint. The post provides a walkthrough of the exploitation process and discusses potential impacts like an attacker being able to access internal resources or exfiltrate credentials from vulnerable Lambda functions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;&lt;table role=&quot;none&quot; width=&quot;100%&quot; border=&quot;0&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=&quot;transparent&quot; style=&quot;background-color:transparent;padding:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;table role=&quot;none&quot; width=&quot;100%&quot; border=&quot;0&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;0&quot;&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; valign=&quot;top&quot; style=&quot;color:#2A2A2A;font-weight:normal;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;&quot;&gt;&lt;h2 style=&quot;color:#2A2A2A;font-weight:normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Supply Chain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td align=&quot;center&quot; valign=&quot;top&quot; style=&quot;font-size:0px;line-height:0px;padding:30px 0px 30px;&quot; class