o carve strings out of remote process memory. This tool allows operators to carve ASCII and UTF-16 strings from targeted processes, making it effective for retrieving JWT tokens, credentials, and other sensitive data directly from memory.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.m5CTo68rhNghfieTCcZktvzLXzATDwVqXkGyJ7Ijf2km6-y1EvKbnQilZ_qRed0q6ELnCUaKExwZNG05Mo42WpvgpGvOil5QCcSJFTpQT1J6KWfeRlXnkN7lCye6oulIk_U8ge8bvwHBBy3oF8wPkRUclpzslqirH5vKsZorxKb0PICKV8RxvYdHTT-guvO7JYPCTsu4oPS58RAH7TUiW6YpoZtxIXLlNF8x5zG-CZhWV-5pXUUu64WbBobcA9iE-9zsxDivCAGvBp_0WZQ6eZhOPoQOfBZT6mmY-y0AP8P5iRuckKrn5_KiHgsnRz-I/4er/A_O0QUTdQvKpB6LLxIdwkg/h42/h001.7fp3dCtzSMeh7mi8XVthzklDiYRw6DHi2hGcuusLPaM&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;hardenedlinux/userland-exec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Userland exec replaces the existing process image within the current address space with a new one. It mimics the behavior of the system call &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;execve&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;, but the process structures describing the process image remain unchanged (the process name reported by system utilities will retain the old process name). This can be used to achieve stealth after gaining arbitrary code execution as well as to execute binaries stored in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;noexec&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; partitions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.f5Zgu5U0wWtAfP7Ta1kAAYonVFocWug6RQW9vuuYUnWHcPSn80lyA1ZnOcZPr433Fm2NJoate7R_KfYvIZ8A5RqZR-V_RijCwQLZxKDaLq1MAr38qTvv81uAidzJrVxdRDOi1nrLb3zJUDY2UpZqweEjQ2fvasfVtJDk7MIMxbduEfBhhvv3gdoJz7dTGN9luFlB9PkAMtrMHBEow7y5cSjcnY955VpRejIKaeAESMJ3yKOb5e43aKcmFzVWSDSd5RQhywlqiBzGULImuZOjdgXpwHxW0fMCi9G5dgGQ6le8m9e4rhOnuda9hfggJeMteH-6WaokIfjVPFHpTIKI9JTljiJgu6N62zBnAM4KJeUY-EdNVkhDyo0r4gGDiJIFtPkav_tfok75Js0c8K1Sacz64esMPfWgGfF7b5elo-o/4er/A_O0QUTdQvKpB6LLxIdwkg/h43/h001.fUsv5FvFMWLwmHaFaPMra11IDLI7GiWwmwDrcn5AnBU&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Abusing VS Code&amp;#39;s Bootstrapping Functionality To Quietly Load Malicious Extensions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.-QQnKmN9995ElcpR565Sh-SDvgyu6m58HhCZ9db8DGKTGB1lJtPVSQSct67uNop-0LCvvCVu90QvjYL4gr5QSssLk_VTkxIrQeFM0eFnTy4MQebICUVXKi587Ya0BdUZl22AdNKMesfAJA_GjBABQiUkAeG-BA8QKJa6HkxiZ7l_ibw4llAbdHTUE8r-hGpvx3co7qgYez509AspwlzM6T8YFADYWkGYbw9nZBPKIhCF5GF-Oj5PKKqE_syY4OH31dXY0KmnggF3IDYhriJ94edK8fLIyAcqQgEkOb0vrjU/4er/A_O0QUTdQvKpB6LLxIdwkg/h44/h001.FE4Prk0FbBlWJyqQ8E22LYJ81JRMXZhs94sNmv_vybY&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Cas van Cooten&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; explores how attackers can abuse VS Code&amp;#39;s bootstrapping and portable installation features to silently install malicious extensions, bypassing normal security prompts (like [0xe2][0x80][0x9c]Do you trust this extension/developer?[0xe2][0x80][0x9d]). By creating ./data and ./bootstrap/extensions folders in a VS Code installation, extensions can be loaded without user interaction. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Cas lists several potential detection methods, but given that normal extensions may call out to the Internet, spawn shells as child processes, etc., looking for anomalous or malicious extension behavior seems tricky.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;&lt;table role=&quot;none&quot; width=&quot;100%&quot; border=&quot;0&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=&quot;transparent&quot; style=&quot;background-color:transparent;padding:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;table role=&quot;none&quot; width=&quot;100%&quot; border=&quot;0&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;0&quot;&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; valign=&quot;top&quot; style=&quot;color:#2A2A2A;font-weight:normal;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;&quot;&gt;&lt;h2 style=&quot;color:#2A2A2A;font-weight:normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;AI + Security&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td align=&quot;center&q